    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Mountain men chose the area of present-day Granger for their annual rendezvous in 1834 to trade furs for goods.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13,
        14,
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Granger%2C_Wyoming"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Granger became a stop on the Union Pacific Railroad's first transcontinental railroad in 1868, which is when it received its name.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Granger%2C_Wyoming"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Pony Express used a local station as a stopover between 1861 and 1862.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Granger%2C_Wyoming"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "A stone and adobe station built in 1856 still stands today and was in operation when Mark Twain passed through.",
